---
description: Guidelines for adding ContextCodeAnalyzer annotations to code
globs: *.cs
alwaysApply: true
---

## Core Principles
- Use structured format with sections: `@purpose`, `@usage`, `@params`, `@returns`, `@notes`
- Write in English for all annotations
- Be concise but informative — each section contains necessary info to understand purpose and usage

## Section Structure
- **@purpose**: Why the class/method/field exists
- **@usage**: When and how to call or use it
- **@params**: Parameter name and description (omit if none)
- **@returns**: What's returned (omit if void)
- **@notes**: Limitations, side effects, implementation specifics

## When to Annotate
- Methods with non-obvious logic, complex behavior, or important side effects
- Parameters/fields that affect class behavior
- Any place where context helps avoid errors

## When to Update
- Logic has changed
- Parameters added/changed
- After refactoring
- When context is not clear enough

## When to Remove
- Code is so simple the purpose is obvious from name/signature
- Context adds no new information
- Method/class is deleted or deprecated

## Example
```csharp
[ContextCodeAnalyzer(
  @purpose: "Attempts to generate a new chip if the generator is charged and free space exists",
  @usage: "Call when generator is charged and a chip needs to be generated",
  @params: "chargeAmount - amount to deduct on generation",
  @returns: "True if chip was generated, false otherwise",
  @notes: "Handles state transitions and chip creation. Sets waiting state if no space."
)]
```
